P8. Q41: do the honoured-law sets nest
======================================

Exhaustive over the whole domain at W = 5, every pair and triple.
Law inventory, 11 laws, two families:
  algebraic : add-comm add-assoc add-ident mul-comm mul-assoc
              mul-ident mul-zero distrib retract-add
  order     : monotone-add absorb-top

configuration            n  honoured
unsigned/wrap/F=0        9  add-comm add-assoc add-ident mul-comm mul-assoc mul-ident mul-zero distrib retract-add
unsigned/wrap/F=1        7  add-comm add-assoc add-ident mul-comm mul-ident mul-zero retract-add
unsigned/saturate/F=0   11  add-comm add-assoc add-ident mul-comm mul-assoc mul-ident mul-zero distrib retract-add monotone-add absorb-top
unsigned/saturate/F=1    9  add-comm add-assoc add-ident mul-comm mul-ident mul-zero retract-add monotone-add absorb-top
signed/wrap/F=0          9  add-comm add-assoc add-ident mul-comm mul-assoc mul-ident mul-zero distrib retract-add
signed/wrap/F=1          7  add-comm add-assoc add-ident mul-comm mul-ident mul-zero retract-add
signed/saturate/F=0      7  add-comm add-ident mul-comm mul-ident mul-zero monotone-add absorb-top
signed/saturate/F=1      7  add-comm add-ident mul-comm mul-ident mul-zero monotone-add absorb-top

Pairwise containment. `<` means the row's set is a strict subset of
the column's, so the column honours strictly more.

                       unsigned/wrap unsigned/wrap unsigned/saturate unsigned/saturate signed/wrap signed/wrap signed/saturate signed/saturate
unsigned/wrap/F=0               =          >          <          X          =          >          X          X
unsigned/wrap/F=1               <          =          <          <          <          =          X          X
unsigned/saturate/F=0           >          >          =          >          >          >          >          >
unsigned/saturate/F=1           X          >          <          =          X          >          >          >
signed/wrap/F=0                 =          >          <          X          =          >          X          X
signed/wrap/F=1                 <          =          <          <          <          =          X          X
signed/saturate/F=0             X          X          <          <          X          X          =          =
signed/saturate/F=1             X          X          <          <          X          X          =          =

NOT every pair is comparable. Q41's option (b) holds over this
inventory: there is a partial order and it is not a ladder.
Incomparable pairs, which are the whole content of that answer:
  unsigned/saturate/F=1  vs  unsigned/wrap/F=0
      only in the first : absorb-top monotone-add
      only in the second: distrib mul-assoc
  signed/wrap/F=0  vs  unsigned/saturate/F=1
      only in the first : distrib mul-assoc
      only in the second: absorb-top monotone-add
  signed/saturate/F=0  vs  unsigned/wrap/F=0
      only in the first : absorb-top monotone-add
      only in the second: add-assoc distrib mul-assoc retract-add
  signed/saturate/F=0  vs  unsigned/wrap/F=1
      only in the first : absorb-top monotone-add
      only in the second: add-assoc retract-add
  signed/saturate/F=0  vs  signed/wrap/F=0
      only in the first : absorb-top monotone-add
      only in the second: add-assoc distrib mul-assoc retract-add
  signed/saturate/F=0  vs  signed/wrap/F=1
      only in the first : absorb-top monotone-add
      only in the second: add-assoc retract-add
  signed/saturate/F=1  vs  unsigned/wrap/F=0
      only in the first : absorb-top monotone-add
      only in the second: add-assoc distrib mul-assoc retract-add
  signed/saturate/F=1  vs  unsigned/wrap/F=1
      only in the first : absorb-top monotone-add
      only in the second: add-assoc retract-add
  signed/saturate/F=1  vs  signed/wrap/F=0
      only in the first : absorb-top monotone-add
      only in the second: add-assoc distrib mul-assoc retract-add
  signed/saturate/F=1  vs  signed/wrap/F=1
      only in the first : absorb-top monotone-add
      only in the second: add-assoc retract-add

The direction, which is the part that answers 76's conjecture:

  unsigned F=0: SATURATING honours strictly more  (9 against 11)
  signed   F=0: neither contains the other  (9 against 7)
  unsigned F=1: SATURATING honours strictly more  (7 against 9)
  signed   F=1: neither contains the other  (7 against 7)

76's candidate put the accuracy-first intent at the TOP and the
speed-first intent's honoured set inside it. The measurement above
says that is RIGHT for unsigned and WRONG for signed, and that
signedness is the dimension deciding which of Q41's options holds.

UNSIGNED. Saturating honours everything wrapping does and two laws
more, monotone-add and absorb-top, which wrapping loses because a
wrap carries a large value back past a small one. So the sets nest,
saturating is on top, and Q41's option (a) holds. That is 76's
direction, established rather than conjectured.

SIGNED. The sets stop nesting. Saturating still gains monotone-add
and absorb-top, and it now LOSES add-assoc, mul-assoc, distrib and
retract-add, because a two-sided clamp is not a semiring congruence
while a one-sided clamp is (see p7). So the two families pull apart
and Q41's option (b) holds: a real partial order that is not a ladder.

So Q41's three options are not competing answers. They are the
answers on different regions, and the predicate separating them is
the signedness. A single ordering over 'the strategies' does not
exist, and the reason is nameable rather than a shrug: the algebraic
family and the order family agree on unsigned and conflict on signed.

What this does not reach: 76's own phrase was chain-level ACCURACY
facts, which are a third family not in this inventory and which order
these configurations by how little they lose rather than by which
identities they satisfy. Nothing here measures that family, so by the
notation nothing here claims anything about it.
